    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d123">

                <pb facs="0226" n="206" sameAs="frus:frus1950v07/pg206"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">795.00/6–2750: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Embassy in India </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-06-27T14:00:00-04:00">June 27, 1950—2 p.
                            m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>642. Madame Pandit was read copy Pres statement re Korea at 11:30 AM, June 27 at
                    Dept. She appeared not too seriously concerned thereby; agreed India had been
                    involved Korean question from beginning; and thought India wld undoubtedly
                    welcome US support of UNSC.</p>
                <p>Although Dept not sanguine Indian reaction will be universally favorable Pres
                    declaration Madame Pandit’s remarks indicate it may be possible vitiate
                    potential original criticism, even win support, by emphasis UN aspects US
                    action.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
